questions.qtext:
Consider a relation $\mathrm{R}(\mathrm{A}, \mathrm{B}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{D}, \mathrm{E})$ with functional dependencies:
$$
\begin{aligned}
&\mathrm{A}, \mathrm{B} \rightarrow \mathrm{C} \\
&\mathrm{C}, \mathrm{D} \rightarrow \mathrm{E}
\end{aligned}
$$
Suppose there are at most $3$ different values for each of $\text{A, B,}$ and $\text{D}.$ What is the maximum number of different values for $\text{E}?$
